  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was Juneteenth\u2014a structural holiday headwind that typically suppresses Saturdays by ~10%. Through that lens, Port Washington crushed it: $2,524 revenue on just 3 groomer-days, running $515/groomer (2% above your 30-day baseline), with transactions up 32% and add-on attach climbing from 40% to 56%. That's operational excellence. Hicksville, conversely, ran only 1 groomer-day and landed at $360/groomer\u20148% below baseline\u2014which explains the 46% total revenue drop. The story isn't underperformance; it's capacity. Hicksville is structurally understaffed.\n\nMonth-to-date, Port Washington sits at $59,597 vs. an expected $60,409 when you adjust for calendar mix\u2014just 1.3% behind, functionally on plan. Hicksville at $20,613 is actually outrunning its DOW-adjusted expectation by 6.6%, suggesting the smaller store is punching above its weight operationally, but it's being choked by groomer availability. Year-to-date Port is up 51% and Hicksville up from a zero baseline; both are growing. The pattern: Port's capacity scaling is working (83 groomer-days MTD vs. 77 last month); Hicksville's is not (42 groomer-days, barely up from 37). Hicksville's visit-cadence drift is your warning light\u2014the 12+ week bucket grew 1.8 percentage points, signaling early-stage churn from undersupply.\n\nOne operational bright spot: retail at Hicksville jumped 83% yesterday despite the grooming shortfall, suggesting front-of-house is engaged. Restock X-Small Treat Tables and Cannoli immediately at both locations\u2014you're running negative inventory.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Port Washington runs 0 groomers today (Sunday dark), 4 tomorrow with 7h bathers; Hicksville runs 0 today, 1 tomorrow; next 7 days Port has 30 groomer-days scheduled, Hicksville 10.**"
